**1. A furnace charging valve assembly, comprising:**

a valve housing defining a charging-material passage and having a valve seat surrounding the charging-material passage;

a shutter configured to engage the valve seat in a closed position and to be movable from the closed position through an unseated position to a retracted position in which the shutter is spaced from the valve seat;

a hollow sleeve shaft supported by the valve housing for rotation about a sleeve-shaft axis, the hollow sleeve shaft including an eccentric portion having an eccentric axis offset from the sleeve-shaft axis;

a tilting shaft extending within and rotatable relative to the hollow sleeve shaft, the tilting shaft being operatively coupled to the shutter through a shutter-support linkage;

a primary actuator operatively coupled to the hollow sleeve shaft and configured to rotate the hollow sleeve shaft through a first angular range, rotation of the hollow sleeve shaft causing the eccentric portion to displace at least a portion of the shutter-support linkage so as to move the shutter substantially away from the valve seat from the closed position to the unseated position without requiring rotation of the tilting shaft relative to the hollow sleeve shaft; and

a secondary actuator operatively coupled to the tilting shaft independently of the primary actuator and configured, after the shutter has been moved to the unseated position, to rotate the tilting shaft relative to the hollow sleeve shaft through a second angular range, rotation of the tilting shaft causing the shutter-support linkage to tilt or swing the shutter from the unseated position to the retracted position remotely spaced from the valve seat,

wherein the eccentric portion of the hollow sleeve shaft and the tilting shaft define nested, independently rotatable drive members that provide respective unseating and retracting motions of the shutter;

wherein the shutter-support linkage is configured to transmit an eccentric displacement generated by the primary actuator as a first motion having a component substantially normal to a sealing surface of the valve seat, and to transmit rotation generated by the secondary actuator as a second motion that moves the shutter laterally away from the charging-material passage; and

wherein the primary actuator and the secondary actuator are controllable in a sequence in which the primary actuator first rotates the hollow sleeve shaft to separate the shutter from the valve seat, the secondary actuator thereafter rotates the tilting shaft to move the shutter to the retracted position, the secondary actuator subsequently returns the shutter to the unseated position, and the primary actuator thereafter rotates the hollow sleeve shaft in an opposite direction to bring the shutter into sealing engagement with the valve seat.

A sealing valve arrangement comprising:

- a valve body defining a flow passage and a sealing seat;
- a shutter configured to sealingly engage the sealing seat;
- an integrated dual-motion mechanism comprising a primary motion assembly and a secondary motion assembly;
- a shutter-carrying tilting arm coupled to the shutter;
- concentric first and second rotational shafts extending about a common rotational axis, the first rotational shaft being operatively coupled to the primary motion assembly and the second rotational shaft being operatively coupled to the secondary motion assembly;
- a first angular actuator configured to rotate the first rotational shaft through a first angular displacement to move the shutter-carrying tilting arm between an open position and a position adjacent the sealing seat; and
- a second angular actuator, independently operable from the first angular actuator, configured to rotate the second rotational shaft through a second angular displacement to tilt the shutter-carrying tilting arm and thereby move the shutter into or out of sealing engagement with the sealing seat,

wherein the primary motion assembly and the secondary motion assembly cooperate to provide independently controllable translational and tilting movement of the shutter.
